### Step 4: Missing values

Some tracts produce `NA`. Before deciding what to do, I checked why. The table lists every tract without a testable change and the reason.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
na_tracts <- poverty_tested %>%
  filter(is.na(z)) %>%
  mutate(
    reason = case_when(
      is.na(`estimate_total_2006-2010`) | `estimate_total_2006-2010` == 0 ~ "No population in 2006–2010",
      is.na(`estimate_total_2013-2017`) | `estimate_total_2013-2017` == 0 ~ "No population in 2013–2017",
      TRUE ~ "Other (needs a closer look)"
    ),
    NAME = str_remove(NAME, ", Philadelphia County, Pennsylvania")
  ) %>%
  select(
    GEOID, NAME,
    pop_2006_2010 = `estimate_total_2006-2010`,
    pop_2013_2017 = `estimate_total_2013-2017`,
    reason
  )

na_tracts %>%
  gt() %>%
  cols_label(
    GEOID = "GEOID", NAME = "Census Tract",
    pop_2006_2010 = "Population\n2006–2010",
    pop_2013_2017 = "Population\n2013–2017",
    reason = "Why NA"
  ) %>%
  tab_header(
    title = "Tracts Excluded from the Significance Test",
    subtitle = "Philadelphia census tracts with no computable poverty rate"
  )
